<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uhtua</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uhtua, now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalevala">Kalevala</a>, is a village in the north of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, on Lake Middle Kuyto. Many of the folk poems that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elias-lonnrot">Elias Lönnrot</a> used for the Kalevala were collected in the surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> Karelia region. In 1919–1920 it was the seat of the Finnish-backed Provisional Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-karelia">East Karelia</a>, and it was renamed Kalevala in 1963.</p></div>
